1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,120
And now for you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:09,020 --> 00:00:17,700
So this guy just celebrated his 50th birthday and he says, you know, my brain has worn out.

3
00:00:17,820 --> 00:00:21,880
There's a lot of miles on it. I can't remember things anymore. I can't remember people's names.

4
00:00:22,320 --> 00:00:25,780
I go running upstairs at my house and I can't remember why I ran up there.

5
00:00:25,780 --> 00:00:28,700
It's time to get a new brain. So he goes down to the brain store.

6
00:00:28,700 --> 00:00:36,160
And there on a lovely shelves on a display are brains in a jar full of alcohol, just like you would imagine.

7
00:00:37,360 --> 00:00:40,960
And he finds this really good looking brain. It's kind of nice and pink.

8
00:00:41,019 --> 00:00:44,860
It looks like it was well taken care of. So he grabs a salesman. He says, hey, how much is this one here?

9
00:00:44,920 --> 00:00:52,100
And the salesman says $250,000, sir. And the guy says, wow, I wasn't really expecting to spend that much.

10
00:00:52,100 --> 00:00:58,000
Why is that one so expensive? And the salesman says, well, this was a college professor who became a Nobel laureate.

11
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:01,700
And this is a really great brain here. He took very good care of it.

12
00:01:02,520 --> 00:01:05,600
And the guy says, no, you know, that's way out of the budget.

13
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:09,060
Let me see what another, what about this one over here?

14
00:01:09,080 --> 00:01:14,180
And he looks at this brain and it's, yeah, it's a little tattered, but it looks like it's probably in better shape than his.

15
00:01:14,580 --> 00:01:16,920
And the guy says, well, that one's only $150,000.

16
00:01:17,940 --> 00:01:21,780
Wow, it's still really expensive. Why is that $150,000?

17
00:01:21,780 --> 00:01:27,400
He says, well, this was a minister, had a large church, a very learned man, very moral.

18
00:01:28,180 --> 00:01:33,640
But, you know, he wasn't quite up to the $250,000 level.

19
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:35,900
Yeah, that's still really expensive.

20
00:01:35,980 --> 00:01:37,360
And the salesman says, well, look around.

21
00:01:37,920 --> 00:01:39,540
Now you have a little background. Just look around.

22
00:01:39,620 --> 00:01:43,560
See if you can find a brain that looks attractive to you that you think will fit.

23
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:45,820
And then let me know.

24
00:01:45,820 --> 00:01:52,380
So the guy looks in all the jars and he comes across this one brain that's kind of small and it's a little tattered.

25
00:01:53,779 --> 00:01:57,399
And it has kind of an unhealthy color to it.

26
00:01:57,479 --> 00:02:00,660
But he thinks, this one's probably in better shape than mine.

27
00:02:01,820 --> 00:02:04,280
So let me see if it's in the budget.

28
00:02:04,880 --> 00:02:06,100
And he calls the salesman over.

29
00:02:06,179 --> 00:02:07,060
He says, how much is this one?

30
00:02:07,100 --> 00:02:09,400
The salesman says, that one is actually $3 million.

31
00:02:10,740 --> 00:02:12,340
Wow, that's expensive.

32
00:02:12,340 --> 00:02:14,600
Why is that so expensive?

33
00:02:14,860 --> 00:02:15,800
$3 million.

34
00:02:16,720 --> 00:02:19,240
It's not as nice as the professor brain.

35
00:02:19,420 --> 00:02:24,660
And the salesman says, well, listen, that one is a scoutmaster's brain.

36
00:02:25,100 --> 00:02:30,960
Do you have any idea how many scoutmasters we have to go through until we can find a brain?

37
00:02:33,620 --> 00:02:34,100
Okay.

38
00:02:35,460 --> 00:02:37,340
Hey, laugh at yourself a little bit, right?

39
00:02:38,260 --> 00:02:39,980
This is podcast number 32.

40
00:02:42,340 --> 00:02:43,780
Hey!

41
00:02:43,780 --> 00:02:43,980
Hey!

42
00:03:08,980 --> 00:03:11,000
Welcome back to the Scoutmaster Podcast.

43
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:12,340
This is Clark Green.

44
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:14,300
Good to have you back.

45
00:03:16,280 --> 00:03:21,080
This edition of the Scoutmaster's Podcast and Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

46
00:03:21,560 --> 00:03:23,780
We're going to talk about a model troop outing.

47
00:03:24,260 --> 00:03:25,100
What happens?

48
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:26,400
What's the role of the leaders?

49
00:03:26,540 --> 00:03:27,840
What's the role of the scouts?

50
00:03:28,840 --> 00:03:30,080
And it's not seven minutes.

51
00:03:30,140 --> 00:03:32,780
It's more than seven minutes, of course, because I can't stop talking.

52
00:03:33,900 --> 00:03:35,460
After that, a quiz.

53
00:03:36,260 --> 00:03:36,640
Yeah.

54
00:03:36,640 --> 00:03:39,339
Right here on the Scoutmaster Podcast.

55
00:03:40,179 --> 00:03:42,480
And it's a quiz about merit badge counselors.

56
00:03:43,100 --> 00:03:44,760
So hopefully you'll find that useful.

57
00:03:45,679 --> 00:03:46,920
That'll about wrap it up.

58
00:03:47,540 --> 00:03:48,859
So we've got a lot to do.

59
00:03:49,220 --> 00:03:50,559
So let's get started, shall we?

60
00:03:51,839 --> 00:03:53,920
Scoutmastership in seven minutes.

61
00:03:54,799 --> 00:03:55,760
Or less.

62
00:03:55,760 --> 00:04:07,880
Any active, lively scout troop is going to go out camping eight to ten times a year.

63
00:04:08,980 --> 00:04:14,120
And they'll do a week of summer camp and hopefully some kind of high adventure activity.

64
00:04:14,780 --> 00:04:17,500
But what happens on these monthly campouts?

65
00:04:17,980 --> 00:04:18,880
What do they look like?

66
00:04:18,940 --> 00:04:19,659
How do they work?

67
00:04:19,920 --> 00:04:20,640
Who does what?

68
00:04:20,640 --> 00:04:27,820
Well, here's a discussion that I want to do in this podcast of a model of these campouts.

69
00:04:27,960 --> 00:04:29,140
Now, this is just a model.

70
00:04:29,560 --> 00:04:31,400
It's just some ideas strung together.

71
00:04:31,640 --> 00:04:38,440
But what it emphasizes is the independence of the youth leadership to do things and to make preparations.

72
00:04:39,380 --> 00:04:41,419
And it kind of describes the adult role.

73
00:04:41,620 --> 00:04:43,380
So let's talk a little bit about preparation.

74
00:04:43,880 --> 00:04:49,640
The major preparation for any outing is going to be done by your patrol leader's counsel.

75
00:04:49,640 --> 00:04:54,200
They've already developed a schedule of events for the year.

76
00:04:55,200 --> 00:04:59,240
And then scouts, individual scouts who are members of that patrol leader's counsel,

77
00:04:59,719 --> 00:05:03,080
may be detailed to set up the particulars of any given camping trip.

78
00:05:03,860 --> 00:05:07,099
A few of the logistics are going to be attended to by adults.

79
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:14,180
And that would mostly involve securing campsites and registering and doing tour permits and things like that.

80
00:05:14,180 --> 00:05:20,000
Although, although, that is, you know, in most troops majorly in adult function,

81
00:05:20,220 --> 00:05:24,240
I would not disallow scouts from becoming involved in things like that.

82
00:05:25,680 --> 00:05:31,000
But in the end, the patrol leader's counsel is responsible for the vast majority of the planning.

83
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:40,300
In the patrols, as they prepare for the trip, they're going to select what we call a grub master.

84
00:05:41,080 --> 00:05:49,560
And this is the person, the scout, who's going to plan, purchase, pack, and prepare the meals for the outing, for the patrol.

85
00:05:49,560 --> 00:05:54,800
The patrol scribe is going to collect fees and permission slips.

86
00:05:55,680 --> 00:06:00,000
And the patrol quartermaster is going to check over the gear that they will need.

87
00:06:00,820 --> 00:06:04,460
And the patrol leader is going to administer all these preparations.

88
00:06:05,540 --> 00:06:07,880
Now, that's the way a good active patrol would work.

89
00:06:07,880 --> 00:06:10,980
Five or six guys in the patrol have a job.

90
00:06:11,840 --> 00:06:14,540
And the patrol leader is going to be administering their jobs.

91
00:06:15,120 --> 00:06:18,180
And they're going to be doing this with the support of the patrol leader's counsel,

92
00:06:18,320 --> 00:06:20,560
the senior patrol leader, the scribe, and the quartermaster.

93
00:06:21,540 --> 00:06:27,040
The senior patrol leader and his cohorts, they're going to form a transportation plan

94
00:06:27,040 --> 00:06:29,620
and oversee the preparation of the patrols.

95
00:06:30,320 --> 00:06:34,740
The troop scribe is going to collect money and permission slips from the patrol scribes

96
00:06:34,740 --> 00:06:37,540
and develop a troop roster for the outing.

97
00:06:37,880 --> 00:06:42,260
The troop quartermaster oversees the patrol quartermasters in preparing their gear.

98
00:06:43,080 --> 00:06:46,820
And the senior patrol leader creates a schedule of activities for the outing.

99
00:06:47,659 --> 00:06:57,900
Now, notice, up to this point, the only adult involvement has been to do those kind of logistical things

100
00:06:57,900 --> 00:07:00,460
that scouts may not be able to do.

101
00:07:01,840 --> 00:07:05,600
And so we're not interfering with this process.

102
00:07:05,920 --> 00:07:07,800
We're letting the scouts go ahead and do it.

103
00:07:08,540 --> 00:07:11,060
They've developed an understanding of how it works.

104
00:07:11,260 --> 00:07:12,659
And they know how to do it.

105
00:07:12,659 --> 00:07:15,780
And they will carry it through.

106
00:07:15,780 --> 00:07:24,119
Now, troop meetings leading up to the outing may discuss special skills or preparations for the trip.

107
00:07:25,080 --> 00:07:28,659
Ample time is scheduled for patrols to complete their preparations.

108
00:07:28,660 --> 00:07:37,820
And so what happens then is that if, like most troops, you leave on a Friday night, Friday night rolls around.

109
00:07:38,820 --> 00:07:44,940
And the SPL and the patrol leaders see that the scouts have their proper personal gear.

110
00:07:44,940 --> 00:07:50,280
And then they make sure they have their patrol and troop gear from the quartermaster.

111
00:07:50,280 --> 00:07:53,940
And see, it's all packed properly and loaded in the vehicles.

112
00:07:54,320 --> 00:07:57,680
And then it's time for the scouts to move out.

113
00:07:57,680 --> 00:08:07,780
Now, the senior patrol leader and his patrol leaders and other members of the patrol leaders council are responsible for making this happen.

114
00:08:07,780 --> 00:08:13,059
And they need to get there a little early, perhaps on a Friday, to make that happen.

115
00:08:13,060 --> 00:08:20,800
Now, once they arrive at the campsite, the senior patrol leader is going to look over the ground and assign areas for each patrol.

116
00:08:20,800 --> 00:08:26,880
He's going to do this with the idea that patrols camp far enough apart to operate independently.

117
00:08:27,300 --> 00:08:32,880
And that the adults that are on the trip are billeted far enough away to allow the troop to function properly.

118
00:08:34,419 --> 00:08:40,600
Before they head off to bed, the patrol leader's council is going to meet and review the schedule for the next day.

119
00:08:42,340 --> 00:08:44,320
So, Saturday morning rolls around.

120
00:08:44,320 --> 00:08:51,320
And the senior patrol leader and patrol leaders are going to get everyone up at the specified hour.

121
00:08:52,180 --> 00:08:54,420
The patrols are going to prepare breakfast.

122
00:08:54,580 --> 00:08:55,740
They're going to cook on their own.

123
00:08:56,260 --> 00:09:02,360
And the adults who've planned and packed their own food were going to observe all this from a respectful distance.

124
00:09:03,100 --> 00:09:06,720
Once breakfast is cleared away and the day is ready to begin,

125
00:09:06,860 --> 00:09:11,300
the senior patrol leader and patrol leaders lead their scouts through whatever activity is the order of the day.

126
00:09:11,880 --> 00:09:14,080
Adults might be invited to offer instruction.

127
00:09:14,320 --> 00:09:18,580
Or might be asked to lend some oversight if the nature of activity demands it,

128
00:09:18,680 --> 00:09:21,500
like swimming or canoeing or something like that.

129
00:09:22,080 --> 00:09:28,240
Otherwise, the adults are pursuing their own activities away from the scouts' activities.

130
00:09:30,620 --> 00:09:38,200
We join in by invitation, not by jumping in and telling the boys how to do things.

131
00:09:38,200 --> 00:09:43,740
We keep ourselves apart, let them go ahead and pursue what they're going to pursue.

132
00:09:44,320 --> 00:09:48,460
Lunch and dinner happen much like breakfast and the day wears on.

133
00:09:48,580 --> 00:09:54,680
And then the entire troop usually gathers for the first time on Saturday evening.

134
00:09:55,140 --> 00:10:00,880
It could be a brief review of the day's events, a campfire, a cracker barrel.

135
00:10:00,880 --> 00:10:03,800
There's lots of different names to put on it.

136
00:10:03,800 --> 00:10:08,480
And we have usually what we call vespers.

137
00:10:08,480 --> 00:10:16,920
And that is just a group meeting in which we talk about what everybody observed and did that day.

138
00:10:17,140 --> 00:10:23,480
And based on the idea of a scout's duty to God and his pledge to be reverend.

139
00:10:23,480 --> 00:10:27,580
And that's usually followed by a cracker barrel and a campfire.

140
00:10:28,740 --> 00:10:37,080
Before everyone retires, the senior patroller reviews Sunday's activities and schedule with the Petroll Leaders Council again.

141
00:10:37,080 --> 00:10:41,320
On Sunday, breakfast, prepared by the patrols.

142
00:10:41,360 --> 00:10:45,360
It might be time to pack up or to pursue some activity before returning home.

143
00:10:46,300 --> 00:10:55,900
But once everyone is packed up and in the vehicles, you're going to arrive back at your place of departure probably.

144
00:10:55,900 --> 00:11:04,640
The senior patrol leader is going to oversee the return of gear with the quartermaster and patrol quartermasters.

145
00:11:05,420 --> 00:11:16,180
And then the patrol leader's council is going to meet soon to discuss the outing and what improvements can be made to their preparations or their implementation.

146
00:11:17,040 --> 00:11:19,220
The gear is going to be checked and cleaned if needed.

147
00:11:19,459 --> 00:11:22,500
And the whole process is going to be ready to begin again.

148
00:11:22,500 --> 00:11:26,420
Now, as I said at the outset, this is just a model.

149
00:11:27,200 --> 00:11:36,760
It's based on the idea that scouts have to be given very wide latitude to operate independent of adult interference, yet with appropriate oversight.

150
00:11:37,360 --> 00:11:43,300
There's very little the scouts can't do for themselves, and they should be encouraged to do as much as they can manage.

151
00:11:44,160 --> 00:11:45,460
Scouts cook on their own.

152
00:11:45,780 --> 00:11:46,960
They camp on their own.

153
00:11:47,080 --> 00:11:49,040
They pursue their activities on their own.

154
00:11:49,040 --> 00:11:53,800
And like I said, by invitation, we may be able to participate actively.

155
00:11:54,360 --> 00:11:57,660
But unless we are invited, we keep a respectful distance.

156
00:11:57,780 --> 00:11:58,819
Now, how far distant?

157
00:11:58,980 --> 00:11:59,620
What do I mean?

158
00:12:01,040 --> 00:12:06,780
Well, far enough away so that we're kind of out of earshot.

159
00:12:06,780 --> 00:12:19,180
That the temptation to jump in and to, quote unquote, help out is tempered by having to walk, you know, 50 paces.

160
00:12:20,380 --> 00:12:21,780
That usually helps me.

161
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:28,319
But close enough that we can be called on very quickly if it's required.

162
00:12:28,320 --> 00:12:44,640
I should note, too, as we're talking about camping, unless a certain outing is designed to be a father or mother and son activity, it's not a family outing.

163
00:12:45,480 --> 00:12:48,300
Scouting happens when the scouts lead themselves.

164
00:12:48,500 --> 00:12:52,380
When they're given the autonomy and trust, they respond by being responsible.

165
00:12:52,380 --> 00:12:54,439
Of course, they will forget things.

166
00:12:55,320 --> 00:13:01,600
Of course, they will not have planned as thoroughly as they need at times.

167
00:13:01,980 --> 00:13:02,980
But we're watching.

168
00:13:03,540 --> 00:13:06,340
We're making sure that this is not a risk to life and limb.

169
00:13:07,060 --> 00:13:15,540
And they will learn exactly the same way that we learned, sometimes by trial and error, sometimes by listening to people who know better.

170
00:13:15,540 --> 00:13:25,140
But when they walk away from it and they have finished their camping trip, they will know it is something that they have done for themselves.

171
00:13:26,099 --> 00:13:28,819
And to me, that's a successful outing.

172
00:13:28,820 --> 00:13:58,800
We'll see you next time.

173
00:13:58,820 --> 00:14:03,380
Well, that music indicates that it's quiz time here on the Scoutmaster Podcast.

174
00:14:04,600 --> 00:14:15,120
Yes, from the very, very long-running and well-known television game show Jeopardy, we have the Think music.

175
00:14:17,560 --> 00:14:22,800
Dum-da-dum-dum-dum-dum-dum-bum-bum.

176
00:14:22,800 --> 00:14:26,860
Hey, I'm no Alex Trebek, but we're going to give this a shot.

177
00:14:27,740 --> 00:14:31,760
This is a quiz about merit badge counselors.

178
00:14:32,380 --> 00:14:33,380
Merit badge counselors.

179
00:14:34,420 --> 00:14:40,740
And I got this off of Bella Online from a fellow called Eric Muller.

180
00:14:41,320 --> 00:14:42,240
I took the quiz.

181
00:14:42,320 --> 00:14:43,060
He created it.

182
00:14:43,600 --> 00:14:46,080
And I think the first time around, I got 8 out of 10 right.

183
00:14:46,080 --> 00:14:49,540
And I learned some things.

184
00:14:50,300 --> 00:14:51,280
It's always possible.

185
00:14:51,760 --> 00:14:52,500
How about that?

186
00:14:53,640 --> 00:14:58,500
So I thought it would be good to put on the podcast, give you something to think about, maybe pick up a few pointers.

187
00:14:59,880 --> 00:15:01,160
It's 10 questions long.

188
00:15:01,260 --> 00:15:02,480
And question number one is,

189
00:15:02,480 --> 00:15:18,600
Well, it is definitely D, all of the above.

190
00:15:18,600 --> 00:15:25,000
It's the responsibility of all parts of the scouting organization to recruit and train merit badge counselors.

191
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,340
Question number two.

192
00:15:27,620 --> 00:15:31,260
All merit badge counselors must be approved by what committee?

193
00:15:31,880 --> 00:15:32,460
What committee?

194
00:15:33,400 --> 00:15:37,360
A, the Boy Scouts of America National Advancement Committee.

195
00:15:38,440 --> 00:15:41,660
B, your council advancement committee.

196
00:15:41,660 --> 00:15:46,360
C, your district advancement committee.

197
00:15:46,819 --> 00:15:52,500
Or D, both your council and district advancement committees.

198
00:15:52,500 --> 00:15:56,400
Well, the answer is D.

199
00:15:57,180 --> 00:15:58,540
There's a pattern developing.

200
00:15:59,720 --> 00:16:06,400
Your district and council advancement committees are going to approve applications from merit badge counselors.

201
00:16:07,060 --> 00:16:08,580
That's an important thing to know.

202
00:16:10,440 --> 00:16:11,100
Question number three.

203
00:16:11,220 --> 00:16:14,760
Which of the following is not true for merit badge counselors?

204
00:16:15,640 --> 00:16:16,500
Not true.

205
00:16:17,600 --> 00:16:19,800
A, they must be a registered scouter.

206
00:16:19,800 --> 00:16:24,300
B, they must be a registered merit badge counselor.

207
00:16:25,300 --> 00:16:28,000
C, they must be 18 or older.

208
00:16:28,900 --> 00:16:33,560
D, they must have skills and education in the subject they are counseling.

209
00:16:35,240 --> 00:16:37,579
The answer is A.

210
00:16:38,540 --> 00:16:43,599
Merit badge counselors are registered under a separate registration.

211
00:16:44,280 --> 00:16:47,479
And they don't have to be registered as a scouting volunteer,

212
00:16:47,480 --> 00:16:50,200
like a scoutmaster, assistant scoutmaster, etc.

213
00:16:50,900 --> 00:16:53,400
But they're registered as a merit badge counselor.

214
00:16:54,560 --> 00:16:56,100
Hey, question number four is,

215
00:16:56,720 --> 00:17:02,020
how many merit badges may one individual counselor counsel?

216
00:17:02,460 --> 00:17:03,440
How much wood?

217
00:17:03,680 --> 00:17:04,740
Would a woodchuck chuck?

218
00:17:04,980 --> 00:17:06,680
If a woodchuck could chuck wood.

219
00:17:07,240 --> 00:17:11,940
So how many merit badges can any single counselor counsel?

220
00:17:11,940 --> 00:17:13,900
Is it A, 3?

221
00:17:14,900 --> 00:17:16,360
B, 5?

222
00:17:17,640 --> 00:17:18,900
C, 7?

223
00:17:19,520 --> 00:17:24,860
Or D, there is no limit to the number an individual counselor can counsel.

224
00:17:26,020 --> 00:17:27,680
So is it 3, 5, 7?

225
00:17:27,920 --> 00:17:29,980
Or basically unlimited?

226
00:17:29,980 --> 00:17:32,200
Well, the answer is D.

227
00:17:32,720 --> 00:17:34,440
Hey, you detected the pattern.

228
00:17:35,260 --> 00:17:36,060
Good for you.

229
00:17:36,940 --> 00:17:40,740
Councils and districts might recommend limits to the number of merit badges

230
00:17:40,740 --> 00:17:42,840
one individual can counsel.

231
00:17:42,980 --> 00:17:45,720
But the Advancement Committee Policies and Procedures Manual

232
00:17:45,720 --> 00:17:52,180
states that there is no limit to the number one person can take on

233
00:17:52,180 --> 00:17:53,480
as a merit badge counsel.

234
00:17:53,480 --> 00:17:57,600
Question number five, we're halfway home.

235
00:17:58,800 --> 00:18:02,640
How many merit badges may a scout take with one merit badge counselor?

236
00:18:05,040 --> 00:18:10,900
So how many merit badges can a scout take from one individual merit badge counselor?

237
00:18:10,900 --> 00:18:13,300
Is it A, 3?

238
00:18:13,500 --> 00:18:14,480
B, 5?

239
00:18:14,720 --> 00:18:15,800
C, 7?

240
00:18:16,400 --> 00:18:19,140
Or D, as many as he likes?

241
00:18:21,260 --> 00:18:22,420
3, 5, 7?

242
00:18:22,840 --> 00:18:24,740
Or D, as many as he likes?

243
00:18:26,420 --> 00:18:27,960
Well, it's D.

244
00:18:28,280 --> 00:18:29,020
It's unlimited.

245
00:18:30,060 --> 00:18:34,040
Once again, a counselor or a district might recommend a limit

246
00:18:34,040 --> 00:18:37,800
to the number of merit badges any one scout takes with any one counselor.

247
00:18:37,800 --> 00:18:42,100
But we've got to look at the Advancement Committee Policies and Procedures Manual

248
00:18:42,100 --> 00:18:43,200
because that's the rules.

249
00:18:44,159 --> 00:18:46,399
And they say that there is no limit.

250
00:18:48,220 --> 00:18:49,600
Here's a tough one.

251
00:18:50,240 --> 00:18:54,520
Question number six, which materials are available to help recruit

252
00:18:54,520 --> 00:18:56,520
and train merit badge counselors?

253
00:18:57,320 --> 00:19:00,680
A, a guide to recommending merit badge counselors.

254
00:19:01,280 --> 00:19:05,040
B, the pamphlet Merit Badge Counseling.

255
00:19:05,040 --> 00:19:10,000
C, merit badge counselor orientation.

256
00:19:10,879 --> 00:19:12,800
Or D, all of the above.

257
00:19:13,960 --> 00:19:16,680
Of course, it's D, all of the above.

258
00:19:17,200 --> 00:19:20,360
There are a lot of publications out there that are available to help recruit

259
00:19:20,360 --> 00:19:21,760
and train merit badge counselors.

260
00:19:22,639 --> 00:19:28,220
And at scouting.org, the Boy Scouts of America official website,

261
00:19:28,440 --> 00:19:31,659
there's a lot of valuable information and training for merit badge counselors.

262
00:19:31,660 --> 00:19:40,500
Question number seven, how often must the merit badge counselor list be renewed?

263
00:19:42,120 --> 00:19:44,140
So there's a list of merit badge counselors.

264
00:19:44,240 --> 00:19:46,200
How often must that be renewed?

265
00:19:46,660 --> 00:19:49,120
One, or A, yearly?

266
00:19:49,120 --> 00:19:52,360
B, every two years?

267
00:19:53,360 --> 00:19:55,399
C, every three years?

268
00:19:56,080 --> 00:20:02,000
Or D, once published, it's valid for so long as counselors wish to remain on the list?

269
00:20:03,959 --> 00:20:07,500
Now you've detected a certain pattern to the answers,

270
00:20:07,659 --> 00:20:09,939
but if you said D this time, you are wrong.

271
00:20:10,260 --> 00:20:11,459
No, it's A, yearly.

272
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:15,600
The merit badge counselor list needs to be reviewed and approved each year.

273
00:20:15,600 --> 00:20:21,740
That way, people who are no longer interested in counseling merit badges won't be on the list.

274
00:20:22,580 --> 00:20:26,060
And new merit badge counselors will make it onto the list.

275
00:20:26,459 --> 00:20:29,820
So that list is going to get reviewed every year.

276
00:20:30,679 --> 00:20:36,240
Question number eight, when can a merit badge counselor counsel his or her own relative?

277
00:20:36,240 --> 00:20:59,100
So which answer?

278
00:20:59,100 --> 00:21:01,780
Well, it was A.

279
00:21:01,780 --> 00:21:04,040
They can counsel a relative at any time.

280
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:11,240
Once again, the Policies and Procedures Manual states that there are no restrictions to counseling a relative,

281
00:21:11,420 --> 00:21:13,660
including your own son, your nephew, your grandchild.

282
00:21:15,320 --> 00:21:19,160
Some counselors might avoid that, but there is no rule against it.

283
00:21:20,160 --> 00:21:21,360
Question number nine,

284
00:21:22,399 --> 00:21:25,720
a scout receives a partial completion for a merit badge at summer camp.

285
00:21:25,720 --> 00:21:26,720
Question number nine,

286
00:21:26,720 --> 00:21:31,300
who can sign off the completion of the merit badge?

287
00:21:32,940 --> 00:21:35,040
Is it A, the scoutmaster,

288
00:21:35,500 --> 00:21:37,420
B, the committee chairman,

289
00:21:38,240 --> 00:21:42,060
C, any counselor approved to counsel the merit badge,

290
00:21:42,400 --> 00:21:44,080
or D, all of the above?

291
00:21:45,860 --> 00:21:47,700
And the answer is

292
00:21:47,700 --> 00:21:51,640
C, any counselor approved to counsel the merit badge.

293
00:21:51,640 --> 00:21:57,820
Only a merit badge counselor approved to counsel the merit badge can sign off on the completion of the merit badge.

294
00:21:58,280 --> 00:22:03,800
Scoutmasters cannot do that unless they are a registered merit badge counselor for the merit badge in question.

295
00:22:04,360 --> 00:22:07,520
Neither can committee chairman or advancement committee chairman.

296
00:22:08,020 --> 00:22:13,580
The only person that can sign off on a merit badge is an approved merit badge counselor.

297
00:22:14,640 --> 00:22:15,300
Number ten,

298
00:22:15,780 --> 00:22:16,480
same scout,

299
00:22:16,820 --> 00:22:19,480
he received a partial completion for a merit badge at summer camp.

300
00:22:19,480 --> 00:22:21,760
How long does he have to complete the merit badge?

301
00:22:23,040 --> 00:22:24,700
Is it A, six months?

302
00:22:25,620 --> 00:22:27,180
B, one year?

303
00:22:27,980 --> 00:22:29,680
C, three years?

304
00:22:30,460 --> 00:22:34,280
Or D, any length of time up until his 18th birthday?

305
00:22:36,440 --> 00:22:39,260
Well, the answer there is back to the old pattern again.

306
00:22:39,340 --> 00:22:41,760
It's D, any length of time until his 18th birthday.

307
00:22:42,460 --> 00:22:44,720
A scout can work on any merit badge,

308
00:22:45,320 --> 00:22:47,060
including partial completions,

309
00:22:47,060 --> 00:22:48,440
for as long as he likes,

310
00:22:48,980 --> 00:22:50,300
until he turns the age of 18.

311
00:22:51,780 --> 00:22:52,639
So how about that?

312
00:22:52,760 --> 00:22:55,560
Those questions really gave me some things to think about.

313
00:22:56,280 --> 00:22:58,700
And hopefully they'll get the juices flowing with you.

314
00:22:58,800 --> 00:23:00,980
You'll start thinking about merit badge counseling

315
00:23:00,980 --> 00:23:02,960
and exactly how it works

316
00:23:02,960 --> 00:23:05,520
and what the policies and procedures are.

317
00:23:06,360 --> 00:23:10,240
And since you've been such a good contestant,

318
00:23:10,240 --> 00:23:12,460
I have a bonus question for you.

319
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:14,020
Question number eleven.

320
00:23:16,700 --> 00:23:20,740
Who reviews merit badges for completeness

321
00:23:20,740 --> 00:23:23,800
and the level of skill that was instructed?

322
00:23:25,080 --> 00:23:28,180
Is it A, the Scoutmaster during a Scoutmaster conference?

323
00:23:28,180 --> 00:23:33,000
B, the Board of Review during a Board of Review?

324
00:23:34,160 --> 00:23:38,860
C, the merit badge counselor before the blue card is signed?

325
00:23:39,640 --> 00:23:44,620
Or D, the District Advancement Committee?

326
00:23:44,620 --> 00:23:49,040
Well, the answer is C,

327
00:23:49,040 --> 00:23:53,500
the merit badge counselor before the merit badge card was signed.

328
00:23:53,719 --> 00:23:56,959
Now, some of the activities and things that a Scout learned

329
00:23:56,959 --> 00:23:58,540
during a merit badge

330
00:23:58,540 --> 00:24:01,060
may be the subject of some questions

331
00:24:01,060 --> 00:24:04,620
or just a discussion at a Board of Review

332
00:24:04,620 --> 00:24:05,919
or a Scoutmaster's conference.

333
00:24:05,920 --> 00:24:09,000
But Scoutmasters or committee people

334
00:24:09,000 --> 00:24:12,580
are not supposed to or allowed

335
00:24:12,580 --> 00:24:17,940
to re-review and re-test merit badges with Scouts.

336
00:24:18,100 --> 00:24:20,180
Once the blue card is signed, it's signed.

337
00:24:20,600 --> 00:24:21,260
And it's done.

338
00:24:21,800 --> 00:24:23,100
I hope that helped you out.

339
